<?php

namespace Database\Seeders;

use App\Models\AiKnowledgeBase;
use Illuminate\Database\Seeder;

class TradingCoachKnowledgeSeeder extends Seeder
{
    public function run(): void
    {
        foreach ($this->articles() as [$module, $difficulty, $title, $content]) {
            AiKnowledgeBase::firstOrCreate(['module' => $module, 'title' => $title], [
                'difficulty' => $difficulty, 'content' => $content, 'status' => 'active',
                'source' => 'HC TradingHub strategy policy', 'language' => 'en',
                'version' => 1, 'reviewed_at' => '2026-10-25',
            ]);
        }
    }

    private function articles(): array
    {
        $structure = AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_MARKET_STRUCTURE;
        $risk = AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_RISK_MANAGEMENT;
        $management = AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADE_MANAGEMENT;
        $psychology = AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_PSYCHOLOGY;
        $styles = AiKnowledgeBase::MODULE_TRADING_STYLES;
        return [
            [$structure, 'beginner', 'Swing Structure and Trend', 'A bullish sequence has higher highs (HH) and higher lows (HL); a bearish sequence has lower highs (LH) and lower lows (LL). A range has repeated rejection around boundaries without a durable directional sequence. Confirm swings using completed candles and the chosen timeframe. Impulses advance structure; corrections retrace it. A protected low in an uptrend or protected high in a downtrend is the swing whose loss would challenge the active thesis. Trend exhaustion is a warning, not proof of reversal.'],
            [$structure, 'intermediate', 'HC Definitions: BOS, CHOCH and MSS', 'HC TradingHub uses BOS for a confirmed candle close beyond a relevant swing in the direction of the current timeframe trend. CHOCH is the first confirmed break of a protected swing against that trend. MSS requires that initial shift and a subsequent confirmed structural continuation. Internal structure can change while external structure remains intact. Liquidity sweeps and false breakouts can cross a level then close back within it. These terms vary across trading methodologies; always state this HC definition and identify the timeframe.'],
            [$structure, 'intermediate', 'H4 H1 M15 Structure Hierarchy', 'H4 supplies major directional context and important levels. H1 is the main tradable internal structure. M15 is the default execution structure. H4 bullish plus H1 bullish plus M15 bearish CHOCH can be a correction; it is not an H1 or H4 reversal. Wait for a relevant H1 support or demand area and confirmed M15 bullish reaction. A touch alone is insufficient. Some presets require a second confirmed retest. If H1 protected structure fails, reassess the bias. Missing timeframe evidence must be stated.'],
            [$structure, 'advanced', 'Trend Transition and Structural Invalidation', 'Separate major from internal trend, and continuation from reversal. A current-timeframe CHOCH against H1 is countertrend until H1 itself invalidates. Assess breakout candle close, retest behavior, protected swing, liquidity sweep, and opposing levels. A broken or invalidated level must not be shown as active. In range-middle conditions, both reward and directional clarity may be poor: No Trade is appropriate.'],
            [$structure, 'beginner', 'Support Resistance and Zones', 'Horizontal support and resistance, supply and demand, equal highs and lows, and psychological prices are potential zones rather than guaranteed reversal prices. Fresh levels are untested; each test can change their quality. Broken support may become resistance (SBR), and broken resistance may become support (RBS), after confirmation. Cluster nearby levels and prioritize levels relevant to the displayed timeframe. Higher-timeframe levels still inform the analysis. Never enter simply because price touches a zone.'],
            [$structure, 'intermediate', 'Breakout Retest and Level Quality', 'A breakout and retest needs a meaningful level, a confirmed close beyond it, a retest that respects the new side, and structure that supports continuation. Fresh levels may support a conditional limit-entry scenario but the reaction is unconfirmed. At tested levels, evaluate confirmed price action before a market-entry scenario. Inspect invalidation, spread, volatility and opposing structure before calling anything executable.'],
            [$structure, 'intermediate', 'Trendlines and Confluence', 'Build bullish trendlines from confirmed rising swing lows and bearish trendlines from confirmed falling swing highs. More clean reactions can strengthen a line; forced anchors weaken it. A trendline is dynamic support or resistance and requires judgment. A break alone is not an entry. Assess timeframe, candle close, retest, structure, nearby horizontal zones and achievable reward versus risk. A false break that returns within the trend invalidates a simplistic breakout reading.'],
            [$risk, 'beginner', 'HC Idea Risk Policy', 'HC TradingHub strategy preference: default risk is 0.5% of account equity per trading idea and the hard maximum is 1%. Related entries and correlated orders representing one idea share the same total budget. Risk is not reset by splitting an idea into multiple positions. A $10,000 account at 0.5% has $50 planned risk. Reject excess risk. Stops can slip, so actual loss may exceed planned loss. No Trade is a valid result. Administrators may make policy more conservative.'],
            [$risk, 'intermediate', 'Position Sizing With Instrument Specifications', 'Monetary budget equals equity times risk percentage. Per-unit planned loss equals stop distance divided by verified tick size times verified tick value and relevant currency conversion, plus estimated per-unit costs. Position size is the remaining idea budget divided by that per-unit loss, rounded down to volume step. Check minimum volume. Forex, gold, CFDs and crypto have different tick and contract specifications; do not assume a universal pip or lot value. Without verified specifications, request them and do not invent size.'],
            [$risk, 'intermediate', 'Drawdown Exposure and Execution Risk', 'Set daily and weekly loss limits and respect overall drawdown rules. Correlated positions can make total exposure larger than it looks. Leverage and margin increase pressure and do not create an edge. Spread, commission, swap, slippage, liquidity, economic news, volatility and trading session all affect execution. Expectancy combines average wins and losses with their frequency; past expectancy does not promise future returns. Martingale and grid recovery can magnify losses and are not HC recommendations.'],
            [$management, 'beginner', 'Structural Stop Loss', 'For a buy idea, structural invalidation may lie below a confirmed swing low, protected higher low, or relevant support/demand zone. For a sell, it may lie above a confirmed swing high, protected lower high, or resistance/supply zone. Consider volatility, ATR when verified, spread, tick size and a sensible buffer. Never move the stop closer merely to manufacture a better R:R. Reduce size when the structural stop is wide; if the minimum size still breaches the cap, reject the scenario.'],
            [$management, 'intermediate', 'Realistic Take Profit and No Trade', 'Select potential take profit from the nearest meaningful opposing support/resistance, prior swing, liquidity area, internal or major structural target, or justified extension. Calculate reward relative to a structural stop only after identifying obstacles. HC prefers at least 1:2 R:R and 1:2.5 to 1:3 when structure allows. Example: buy 2650, structural stop 2640, but strong resistance 2664 gives only 1.4R; do not extend TP to 2670 through that obstacle solely to claim 2R. Wait for a better entry or No Trade.'],
            [$management, 'intermediate', 'Break-Even at One R or Opposing Level', 'At +1R, or when price reacts to a meaningful opposing level, evaluate moving a stop toward break-even. This is a choice, not an automatic rule: normal pullbacks may stop the position early. Nominal entry-price BE differs from true cost-adjusted BE, which accounts for spread, commission, swap and execution costs. A small-profit stop is a separate choice. Slippage or a gap means no stop guarantees zero loss. Review whether the original structural thesis remains valid.'],
            [$management, 'advanced', 'Trade Management Through Structural Change', 'Below +1R, first assess whether original invalidation still holds. At +1R, +2R and +3R, compare the next structural obstacle with continuation evidence; consider holding, partial protection or no action. A successful break and retest can support continuation, while internal reversal may be a correction and major reversal challenges the thesis. Ranging conditions and elevated volatility call for reassessment. Explain the advantage and cost of each option without assigning invented probabilities.'],
            [$psychology, 'beginner', 'FOMO Revenge Trading and Cooling Off', 'FOMO can turn a missed setup into an impulsive entry. After consecutive losses, increasing lot size to recover is revenge trading and violates consistent risk. Pause, review the setup and Trading Journal, check daily loss limits, and use a cooling-off period. Recovery is never guaranteed. Keep each new idea within the configured 0.5% default and 1% ceiling.'],
            [$psychology, 'intermediate', 'Bias and Execution Discipline', 'Overconfidence, confirmation bias, recency bias, loss aversion and emotional attachment can distort evidence. Greed can delay exits; fear can close winners early or cause hesitation. Moving a stop farther away after entry changes the original risk thesis. Review both winning and losing decisions against a written plan, including impulse, overtrading, unrealistic expectations, and rule breaks. Process quality is distinct from one outcome.'],
            [$styles, 'beginner', 'Scalping Intraday Swing and Position Styles', 'Scalping uses short holds and lower-timeframe execution, so spread, liquidity and fast decisions matter. Intraday trading closes within a day and uses session conditions and multi-timeframe structure. Swing trades may last days, use wider structural stops and face overnight/weekend risk. Position trades may last much longer and emphasize daily/weekly structure and fundamental context. No style is always profitable; choose one whose time commitment, costs and risk fit the trader.'],
            [$styles, 'intermediate', 'HC Default Strategy and Setup Families', 'The HC default strategy uses H4 major direction, H1 internal structure, M15 execution, support and resistance as core levels, trendlines as confirmation and EMA 24/52/100/200 as optional secondary confirmation. Entries require structure confirmation. Strategies include trend following, countertrend, range, breakout and retest, support/resistance rejection, pullback continuation, trendline reaction, EMA confirmation and confluence. Label a trade against H1 as countertrend and explain added risk. Apply 0.5% default idea risk, 1% hard maximum and realistic 1:2 minimum target.'],
        ];
    }
}
